course: dictionary
lesson: 2489
title: Deaf: two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context shows which one the writer means. Deaf usually means not able to hear, or not able to hear well. «Dogs also work for us: they guide blind people, warn deaf people about sounds and can even smell some illnesses.» The clue is warn about sounds: deaf people may not hear those sounds well, so the dogs help them.
explain: Deaf can also mean not willing to listen. «He was deaf to our requests.» The word requests tells you that people asked him for something, and he would not listen or respond. So here deaf has nothing to do with ears.
explain: Always test your meaning in the sentence. Try swapping in «not able to hear well» or «not willing to listen» and see which one makes sense.
fact: Deaf means not able to hear, or not able to hear well.
fact: Deaf to something means not willing to listen to it, as in «He was deaf to our requests.»
fact: Phrases such as «fall on deaf ears» and «turn a deaf ear» are about people refusing to listen.
fact: Deaf people may use sign language, lip reading, hearing aids or cochlear implants.
fact: People who are part of Deaf culture and use sign language often write Deaf with a capital D.
exercise: «Dogs also work for us: they warn deaf people about sounds.» What does deaf mean here? | *Not able to hear well | Very angry | Asleep | Not willing to listen
exercise: Lucy says her brother was «deaf to our requests» because he kept playing. What does deaf mean in that sentence? | *Not willing to listen | Not able to hear at all | Very quiet | Happy to help
exercise: «Automatic captions sometimes mishear, which matters a lot for deaf viewers.» Who do captions matter most to here? | Dogs | Capercaillies | *Deaf viewers | Grandad
test: «People who are part of Deaf culture and use sign language often write Deaf with a capital D.» What do they often do? | *Write Deaf with a capital D | Write in red ink | Stop using sign language | Use hearing aids only
test: «The capercaillie is the biggest grouse, and it is now rare.» What does the sentence tell you about the capercaillie? | It lives in the sea | It can talk to people | It is the smallest grouse | *It is now rare
exam: Which sentence uses deaf to mean not willing to listen? | Automatic captions help deaf viewers | Deaf people may use hearing aids | Dogs warn deaf people about sounds | *He was deaf to our requests
